<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Croesus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 560–<a href="/years/546-bce">546 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Croesus of Lydia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Croesus was the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, ruling in the sixth century BCE and proverbial for his wealth. He was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-thymbra">Battle of Thymbra</a> in 546 BCE, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> was captured. His fall brought Lydia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greek</a> cities under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/croesus.jpg" alt="Croesus" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marco Prins · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Croesus_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He then defeated <b>Croesus of Lydia</b>, traditionally at the Battle of Thymbra, and captured Sardis around 547–546 BCE, bringing the Ionian Greek cities of the Aegean coast under Persian suzerainty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Croesus of Lydia</b> sent gold and silver offerings, and cities built treasuries along the Sacred Way, such as the Siphnian Treasury of about 525 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 560 BCE <b>Croesus of Lydia</b> besieged the city; according to Herodotus, the Ephesians placed their town under the protection of Artemis by stretching a rope from the walls to the temple, some seven stades away, and then came to terms with him.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Later, separate pure gold and silver coins were struck, traditionally credited to <b>Croesus</b>, although some scholars date the first of these to the early Persian period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gordium site shows a considerable building programme in the 6th century BCE under Lydian kings including the proverbially rich <b>Croesus</b>, and in Seleucid times Antiochus III settled 2,000 Jewish families from Mesopotamia and Babylon in Lydia and Phrygia with land, tax exemptions and vineyards.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>When Cyrus the Great conquered Lydia in 546 BCE, Miletus alone among the Ionian cities obtained a treaty on the same terms it had held with <b>Croesus</b>, and so escaped the Persian conquest that followed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.49,28.04&amp;year=-553" aria-label="Sardis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5247 782 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Croesus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5697 1052h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5697 1052h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=38.49,28.04&amp;year=-553">What was here in 553 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
